{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And HaShem Elohim planted a garden in Eden, from of old</b> (מִקֶּדֶם). Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 2:8) explained: in the east of Eden He planted the garden. And Onkelos (Targum Onkelos on Genesis 2:8) translated מִלְּקַדְמִין (\"from aforetime\"), and so they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 15:3), and this is the correct interpretation. And the meaning of \"and He planted\" is not that He brought the trees from another place and planted them there, for from that very place He caused them to grow, as it says, \"and HaShem Elohim caused to grow\" etc. Rather, the sense of \"and HaShem Elohim planted\" is to declare that they were the planting of HaShem, for before He decreed upon the earth, \"Let the earth bring forth vegetation\" (Genesis 1:11), He decreed concerning that place that there would be a garden there, and said, \"Here shall be such-and-such a tree, and here such-and-such a tree,\" like a planted bed (כַּעֲרוּגַת הַמַּטָּעִים); and it was not like the manner of the other places of the earth, concerning which He said, \"Let the earth bring forth vegetation, fruit trees\" (Genesis 1:11), and it sprouted without order. Now the trees of the Garden of Eden — He decreed concerning them that they should produce foliage and bear fruit forever: in the earth their root does not age, and in the dust their trunk does not die (cf. Job 14:8–9). They require neither one who works (עוֹבֵד) nor one who prunes (זוֹמֵר), for if they required labor, after the man was banished from there, who tended them? This too is the meaning of \"and HaShem Elohim planted\": that His plantings are the work of His hands and endure forever, as it says (Ezekiel 47:12), \"Its leaf shall not wither, neither shall its fruit fail\" etc., \"because their waters issue from the Sanctuary.\" If so, what is the meaning of \"and He placed him in the Garden of Eden to work it and to keep it\" (Genesis 2:15)? He placed him there to sow for himself wheat and kinds of produce, and every herb bearing seed, and beds of spices, and to reap and to pluck and to eat as he wishes. And this is the meaning of \"to work it and to keep it\" — to work the soil of the garden, the beds that he would make there, for the garden — which is the trees — does not require working. And it is possible that \"garden\" (גַּן) is used in the feminine, as in \"and like a garden (וּכְגַנָּה) whose seeds shoot forth\" (Isaiah 61:11), \"and they shall plant gardens (גַּנּוֹת)\" (Jeremiah 29:5). And our Rabbis were moved by this matter; they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 16:5): \"'To work it and to keep it' (לְעָבְדָהּ וּלְשָׁמְרָהּ) — these are the offerings, as it says (Exodus 3:12), 'you shall serve (תַּעַבְדוּן) the Elohim,' and thus it is written (Numbers 28:2), 'you shall keep (תִּשְׁמְרוּ) to offer to Me.'\" And their intention in this is to say that the plants and all living beings require the supernal forces, and from them comes their growth, and through the offerings there will be a drawing down of blessing to the supernal realms, and from them to the plants of the Garden of Eden, and through them they will exist and live forever, through rains of favor and blessing that they draw into it. As they said (Bereshit Rabbah 15:1): \"'The trees of HaShem are sated, the cedars of Lebanon, which He planted' (Psalms 104:16) — Rabbi Chanina said: they are sated with their life (חַיֵּיהֶן), they are sated with their waters (מֵימֵיהֶן), they are sated with their planting (מַטַּעְתָּן).\" For \"their life\" (חַיֵּיהֶן) is their supernal foundations, and \"their waters\" (מֵימֵיהֶן) is the good storehouse that brings down the rain, and \"their planting\" (מַטַּעְתָּן) is their power in the heavens, as they said (Bereshit Rabbah 10:6): there is no herb below that does not have a mazal in the firmament, and it strikes it and says to it, \"Grow!\" as it says (Job 38:33), \"Do you know the statutes of heaven, or do you impose its rule on earth?\" — שׁוֹטֵר."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The word שׁוֹטֵר at the very end appears to be a scribal notation (\"check\" or \"collated\"), here translated literally as it stands in the text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:24:17.382786973Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}